      <h3 id="whats-live">1. What's live on your domain</h3>
      <p>Your site runs on your own domain, on a fast global hosting network, with a secure connection (HTTPS) and the latest web protocols. Google received your sitemap on launch day, and Bing usually picks it up from Google within about two weeks. Here's what's live:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pages.</strong> Home, About, Services (with a section for each offering), Pricing or Quote, Service Area pages (one for each region you cover), Contact, Booking (if you have it), and your blog.</li>
        <li><strong>Operational pages.</strong> Privacy Policy, Terms of Use, Accessibility Statement, Sitemap (a page for people and an XML file for search engines), and Style Guide. Every site needs these, even if few visitors open them.</li>
        <li><strong>Files for search engines.</strong> robots.txt, sitemap.xml, an RSS feed (feed.xml), structured data (JSON-LD) on every page, link-preview tags for social media and chat apps (Open Graph and Twitter Card), and canonical URLs.</li>
        <li><strong>Search.</strong> A search box that covers every page and runs entirely in the visitor's browser, with no server, no cookies, and no outside API call.</li>
        <li><strong>Forms.</strong> One or more contact and quote forms, connected to a form service that emails each submission straight to you. I keep no copy.</li>
        <li><strong>Site icons.</strong> A full favicon set (for every device, every browser, and both dark and light modes), generated automatically.</li>
      </ul>

