      <h3 id="asking-for-changes">3. How to ask for changes</h3>
      <p>The quickest way is a plain email or text. You don't need to write it like a support ticket. All four examples below get the same response time.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>"Our new hours are 7am to 6pm Monday through Saturday."</li>
        <li>"Add a new service page for gutter cleaning. Same layout as drain cleaning."</li>
        <li>"Please swap out the team photo on the About page. New one attached."</li>
        <li>"Grand Opening special. Can we put a banner at the top for two weeks?"</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>How to send it:</strong> email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}" class="inline-link">{{ site.email }}</a>, call or text <a href="tel:{{ site.phone }}" class="inline-link">{{ site.phoneDisplay }}</a>, or use the <a href="/content-update/" class="inline-link">content update form</a>. All three reach my inbox.</p>
      <p><strong>Turnaround and cost.</strong> Routine edits, such as a typo, a phone number, a price change, or a new staff bio, are included in the plan, and they go live quickly on most business days. Work outside the plan, such as a custom calculator, a booking system from another company, or a new feature on the site, costs $100 an hour, and I send a written estimate before I start.</p>

      <h3 id="emergencies">4. What counts as an emergency</h3>
      <p>An emergency is anything visitors can see that's wrong, dangerous, or broken. Here are three kinds I drop everything for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The site is down.</strong> Visitors get a server error (5xx), a blank page, or a DNS failure.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrong information is live.</strong> A wrong phone number, address, or price on an active promotion, or out-of-date hours on a holiday weekend.</li>
        <li><strong>A security or privacy concern.</strong> A defaced page, suspicious form submissions, anything that looks like an attack, or a customer reporting a data leak.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What to do.</strong> Don't wait to fill in a form. Call or text <a href="tel:{{ site.phone }}" class="inline-link">{{ site.phoneDisplay }}</a> directly, and I'll answer or call you back as fast as I can. The <a href="/emergency/" class="inline-link">emergency form</a> works too, because it alerts me the same way.</p>
      <p><strong>What isn't an emergency.</strong> A new service page (free within your ten pages, $100 each after that), a wording change, a photo swap, or a missing comma goes through the regular content-update channel instead.</p>

      <h3 id="form-submissions">5. Form submissions</h3>
      <p>Every contact, quote, or booking form on your site sends straight to a form service, which emails the submission to the address I set at launch (usually an inbox you already check). There's no dashboard to log into, and no copy is stored on my side or yours.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Where they go.</strong> The address or addresses on file, usually owner@yourdomain or office@yourdomain.</li>
        <li><strong>Spam protection.</strong> A hidden honeypot field and a limit on how often each form can be sent, with no CAPTCHA. I check every week for messages blocked by mistake.</li>
        <li><strong>Adding or changing recipients.</strong> Send me one email, and I'll make the change.</li>
        <li><strong>Auto-reply.</strong> Visitors get an instant "I got it" email with your phone number. You can change the wording any time.</li>
        <li><strong>Reading the data.</strong> Submissions arrive as plain-text email, with every field labeled. No login needed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="your-domain">6. Your domain belongs to you</h3>
      <p>You bought it, it's registered in your name, and you control it. I don't register domains for clients, because that would put control of yours with me on the day you need it most. Here's what I do:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Configure DNS.</strong> I point the records at your hosting. The records sit at your registrar, where you can see them any time.</li>
        <li><strong>Remind you to renew.</strong> I send reminders 60, 30, and 7 days before the domain expires. You pay the renewal, not me. I watch the expiration date in the public WHOIS registry myself, so the reminder doesn't depend on your registrar's email.</li>
        <li><strong>Help you find it.</strong> If you've lost track of where you registered the domain, search your inbox for "domain renewal" or "ICANN whois". The registrar's name will be in those emails.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If your domain ever lapses, the site goes offline within 24 to 48 hours. For most domain endings, you can recover a lapsed domain within a 30-day grace period, though the registrar may charge a redemption fee. The 60-day reminder is there so this never happens.</p>

      <h3 id="email">7. Email on your domain</h3>
      <p>Email is separate from your website, even though they share a domain. I don't host email; I set the DNS records that tell the world where your email lives. Most owners choose one of these:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Google Workspace.</strong> $7.20 per user per month, for the best inbox, calendar, and file storage. I set the MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records at no charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Microsoft 365.</strong> $7.20 per user per month, set up the same way.</li>
        <li><strong>Forwarding.</strong> Free. Mail to <code>you@yourdomain.com</code> goes on to your existing Gmail or other inbox. It's a good choice if you only need a professional address and already have an inbox that works.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>I never lock your email behind me, charge to "configure" DNS, or move your email when you change hosts. The records are at your registrar, and they're yours.</p>

      <h3 id="hosting-uptime">8. Hosting and uptime</h3>
      <p>Your site runs on a global hosting network, served from more than 300 locations worldwide. For most U.S. visitors, the closest one answers within 50 ms. The uptime target is 99.99%, and the setup is designed to make long outages very rare.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Monitoring.</strong> Uptime checks every minute from three regions, plus the hosting network's own status feed. I hear about a problem before you notice it.</li>
        <li><strong>What "down" looks like.</strong> Most outages affect one region, not the whole network, so even during an incident your site usually stays up for most visitors.</li>
        <li><strong>Bandwidth.</strong> Unlimited, with no overage charges.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="security-backups">9. Security and backups</h3>
      <p>There's no admin panel, no database, no plugins, and no PHP. All an attacker could target is a set of static files on a global network. In detail:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No login form anywhere on your site.</strong> There's no password to guess.</li>
        <li><strong>No database to break into.</strong> Forms send to an outside service, not to a server I maintain.</li>
        <li><strong>HTTPS only.</strong> Eligible for browsers' HSTS preload list, with a 12-month max-age and includeSubDomains.</li>
        <li><strong>Security headers.</strong> Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, and Permissions-Policy on every response.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Backups.</strong> Every change I make is saved as its own backup, and your site's code lives in a private repository. I can restore any earlier version in under five minutes by undoing a change and republishing. If your own computer fails on the day you ask for a rollback, it doesn't matter, because the full history is in the repository.</p>

      <h3 id="seo-foundation">10. SEO foundation</h3>
      <p>SEO has two parts: the technical foundation, which a site either has or doesn't, and ongoing content, which builds over time. I build the foundation, and you control the content.</p>
      <p><strong>What I built in:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Clean, semantic HTML, one H1 per page, and headings in order.</li>
        <li>A title and description for each page (50 to 160 characters, naming your service and city).</li>
        <li>Structured data (JSON-LD): LocalBusiness on the site, Service on each service page, Article on each blog post, and FAQPage on the FAQ.</li>
        <li>Link-preview tags (Open Graph and Twitter Card), so links shared on social media show a proper preview.</li>
        <li>An XML sitemap, submitted to Google Search Console at launch.</li>
        <li>Core Web Vitals well inside Google's "good" range on real visitor data: LCP under 1.5 s, CLS under 0.05, and INP under 100 ms.</li>
        <li>Mobile-first design (Google indexes the mobile version of a site first).</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What I don't promise:</strong> a specific ranking position. No one controls Google's rankings, so no one can promise one. What I do promise is that your technical foundation will never hold your rankings back.</p>

      <h3 id="analytics">11. Visitor statistics</h3>
      <p>Two visitor-statistics tools run on your site by default. Both are cookie-free and GDPR-compliant by design, so neither needs a cookie banner.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hosting-network statistics.</strong> Real User Monitoring on the hosting network sees every visit without a script on the page, and it tells me your real-world Core Web Vitals across all your visitors.</li>
        <li><strong>Your visitor dashboard.</strong> Included with every monthly plan. I set up a private dashboard for your site and send you the live link on launch day. It shows page views, referrers, devices, top pages, and bounce rate, with no personal data and no cookies. It's your dashboard and your numbers, so check it any time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What I add only if you ask:</strong> Google Analytics, the Facebook Pixel, the LinkedIn Insight Tag, the TikTok Pixel, or any other advertising script. They slow the site, need a cookie banner, and most of them get blocked anyway.</p>

      <h3 id="google-business">12. Google Business Profile</h3>
      <p>Your Google Business Profile is separate from your website but closely connected to it. I don't manage it, because Google flags a profile that a third party holds for long. Instead, I connect your site to it: the structured data on your site matches your profile (name, address, phone, hours, and services), so Google treats them as the same business.</p>
      <p>Keeping the profile current is your job, and a 15-minute checklist each quarter covers it: hours, photos, replies to reviews, and one post. Run through it on the first Monday of each quarter. Over a year, it adds up to a noticeable rise in Google's map results (the "local pack").</p>

      <h3 id="adding-content">13. Adding pages, posts, or content</h3>
      <p>New content is part of what the monthly plan covers. Most additions are included, and work outside the plan costs $100 an hour, with a written estimate before I start.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Edits to existing pages.</strong> Included in the plan.</li>
        <li><strong>New blog posts.</strong> The blog is part of your plan. Send me a draft, and I'll edit, lay out, and publish it as part of your unlimited content updates.</li>
        <li><strong>A new content page within scope.</strong> Send rough copy and photos, and I'll build and publish the page as part of your unlimited updates. Your plan includes ten pages, and each page beyond the ten costs $100.</li>
        <li><strong>A new region or service-area page.</strong> Send the city name and a paragraph of local detail (which ranks better than generic copy), and the page is part of your unlimited updates.</li>
        <li><strong>A new feature or connection.</strong> Custom calculators, booking systems from other companies, email-marketing templates, and similar work cost $100 an hour, quoted before I start.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="seasonal">14. Seasonal and scheduled updates</h3>
      <p>You can schedule changes ahead of time. Here are three kinds I often set up for clients:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Promo banners that end on their own.</strong> Tell me "this banner runs from Dec 1 to Dec 24," and I'll schedule the start and the end. You don't have to remember a thing.</li>
        <li><strong>Holiday hours.</strong> Send your list of closures once a year. I post each closure 48 hours ahead and remove it afterward.</li>
        <li><strong>Seasonal services.</strong> Snow removal from November to March, lawn care from April to October: tell me your dates, and I'll hide and show the page automatically.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="invoice">15. Reading your monthly invoice</h3>
      <p>Most months, your invoice is one line. If a charge ever looks wrong, email me and I'll explain it or remove it. No billing question has ever taken more than one email to settle.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Monthly plan: $175.</strong> Covers hosting, the security certificate, monitoring, unlimited content updates, the blog, and direct support. It's the same amount every month.</li>
        <li><strong>Hourly work outside the plan (rare).</strong> Listed below the plan line at $100 an hour, only when you've asked for work outside the plan and I've sent a written estimate first.</li>
        <li><strong>Pass-through costs (rare).</strong> If I paid a third party for you, such as for a stock photo or a paid font, it appears here at cost, with no markup.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="leaving">16. If you ever want to leave</h3>
      <p>Here's what happens if you cancel. After the 12-month minimum, you can cancel with 30 days' written notice. Within the 12-month minimum, cancelling early means paying the remaining monthly installments.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Your domain.</strong> Already registered in your name, and you control it. I help you move it to a registrar of your choice and unlink it from my hosting, at no charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Your website.</strong> On the monthly plan, I own the code. The site goes offline at the end of the term. There's no export of the site and no code buyout.</li>
        <li><strong>Your photos, text, and brand assets.</strong> Yours, as they've always been.</li>
        <li><strong>Your forms.</strong> The form service account is in your name, or I can move it to your name with one email.</li>
        <li><strong>Your inboxes.</strong> Already yours. I never had access.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>There's no exit fee and no surprise final invoice.</p>

